## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical IPC with shared memory and demonstrates mastery of memory ordering in a real, implementable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- Single-producer/single-consumer lock-free design
- Atomic operations and acquire/release semantics
- Memory visibility across cores and cache-line padding
- mmap-based IPC and data layout in shared memory

## Code Example
```c
// Simplified sketch (not production-ready)
typedef struct {
  uint64_t head; // read index
  uint64_t tail; // write index
  char data[4096];
} ring_t;

void prod(ring_t *r, const void *src, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // write data to slot (omitting slot indexing details)
  memcpy(r->data + (t % 1024) * 4, src, n);
  __atomic_store_n(&r->tail, t+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
}

void cons(ring_t *r, void *dst, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  uint64_t h = __atomic_load_n(&r->head, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  if (h < t){
    memcpy(dst, r->data + (h % 1024) * 4, n);
    __atomic_store_n(&r->head, h+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple producers? discuss ring partitioning or a per-slot sequence counter.
- What changes for varying message sizes or overflow handling? discuss padding and data-copy costs.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests lock-free IPC design, precise memory ordering, and verification under contention.

## Key Concepts

- lock-free primitives, per-slot sequencing, memory barriers, mmap-based IPC, cache coherence.
- detection of partial writes and ensuring visibility under contention.

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t seq; char payload[256]; } slot_t;

void write_slot(slot_t *s, void *data, size_t n){
  __atomic_store_n(&s->seq, 0, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
  memcpy(s->payload, data, n);
  __atomic_store_n(&s->seq, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
}

bool read_slot(slot_t *s, void *out){
  uint64_t a = __atomic_load_n(&s->seq, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  if (a != 1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, 256);
  uint64_t b = __atomic_load_n(&s->seq, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  return (a == b && a == 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of synchronization primitives and memory ordering in a toy OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Barrier reuse via a generation counter
- Race-free wakeups with a condition variable analogue
- Memory visibility and minimal locking
- Testing for progress and fairness under contention

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int count;
  int needed;
  int gen;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
  pthread_cond_t cv;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) {
  b->count = 0;
  b->needed = n;
  b->gen = 0;
  pthread_mutex_init(&b->m, NULL);
  pthread_cond_init(&b->cv, NULL);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&b->m);
  int g = b->gen;
  if (++b->count == b->needed) {
    b->count = 0;
    b->gen++;
    pthread_cond_broadcast(&b->cv);
  } else {
    while (b->gen == g) {
      pthread_cond_wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&b->m);
}
```

